'''warfare''' (wâr′fãr), ''n.'' [Early mod. E. ''fare''; < ''war''<sup>1</sup> + ''fare''<sup>1</sup>.] '''1.''' A warlike or tary expedition; military operations; h ties; war; armed contest.
{{smaller block|What iniurie doth the Prince to the Captein sendes him a ''warrefare'', if he makes him sure to h victorie? ''Guevara'', Letters (tr. by Hellowes, 1577)
The Philistines gathered their armies together f ''fare.''
{{right|1 Sam. xx}}}}
'''2.''' Figuratively, any contest, struggle, or s
{{smaller block|The weapons of our ''warfare'' are not carnal. 2 C
How truly a ''warfare'' is this life, if the king heaven itself have not this peace in perfection!
{{right|''Donne'', Sermo}}}}

'''warfare''' (wâr′fãr), ''v. i.'' [< ''warfare'', ''n.''] carry on warfare or engage in war; con struggle.
{{smaller block|He that can apprehend and consider vice with baits and seeming pleasures, and yet abstain, and y tinguish, and yet prefer that which is truly bette the true ''warfaring'' Christian. ''Milton'', Areopa}}

'''warfarer''' (wâr′fãr-ėr), ''n.'' One engaged in or in a contest or struggle of any sort.

'''warfaring''' (wâr′fãr-ing), ''n.'' The act of c ing on war. [Rare.]
{{smaller block|The Burg of the Niblung people and the heart of ''warfaring.''
