'''wark<sup>1</sup>''' (wärk), ''v. i.'' [< ME. ''werken'', ''warchen'', < AS. ''wærcian'' (= Icel. ''verkja'', ''virkja''), pain: see ''wark''<sup>1</sup>, ''n.''] To be in pain; ache.

'''wark<sup>2</sup>''' (wärk), ''n.'' A dialectal (Scotch) form of ''work.''

'''warkamoowee''' (wär-ka-mö′wē), ''n.'' [Cingalese.] A canoe with outriggers, used at Point de Galle, island of Ceylon. {{smaller|It is generally manned by four or five lascars, who sit grouped together at the}}
[[FIGURE]]
{{c|{{smaller|Warkamoowee of Point de Galle.}}}}
{{smaller|end of the lever, adding or taking away a man according to the strength of the wind. The warkamoowees, during the northeast monsoon, even when it is blowing very hard, venture 20 or 25 miles from land for the purpose of fishing, or to carry fruits to vessels in the offing. They often sail 10 miles an hour.}}

'''warkand†''', ''a.'' [ME. also ''warchond''; pp. of ''wark'', ''v.''] Painful.

'''warkloom''' (wärk′lüm), ''n.'' A tool; an instrument. [Scotch.]

