{{smaller block|<poem>Where is this ''warlowe'' with his wande,
That wolde thus wynne oure folke away?</poem>
{{right|''York Plays'', p. 81.}}
<poem>Ye’re but some witch or wil ''warlock'',
Or mermaid o’ the flood.</poem>
{{right|''The Lass of Lochroyan'' (Child’s Ballads, II. 109).}}}}
{{smaller block|It seems he [Æneas] was no ''Warluck'', as the Scots commonly call such men, who, they say, are iron-free, or lead-free. {{right|''Dryden'', Epic Poetry.}}}}
'''3†.''' A monster.
{{smaller block|<poem>Loke of lyuyaton [leviathan] in the lyffe of saynt Brandon,
There this ''warloghe'', I wis, a water eddur is cald,
That this saint there seghe in the se occiane.</poem>
{{right|''Destruction of Troy'' (E. E. T. S.), l. 4439.}}}}

'''warlock<sup>2</sup>†''' (wâr′lok), ''n.'' [ME. ''warlok'', ''warloc''; < ''war-'' (uncertain) + ''lock''<sup>1</sup>.] A fetterlock.
{{smaller block|''Warlok'', a fetyr lok (''warloc'' of feterloc, P.), Sera pedicalis, vel compedicalis (compedalis, S. P.).
{{right|''Prompt. Parv.'', p. 517.}}
<poem>I com wyth those tythynges, thay tame bylyue,
Pynez me in a prysoun, put me in stokkes,
Wrythe me in a ''warlok'', wrast out myn yȝen.</poem>
{{right|''Alliterative Poems'' (ed. Morris), iii. 80.}}}}

'''warlockry''' (wâr′lok-ri), ''n.'' [< ''warlock''<sup>1</sup> + ''-ry'': see ''-ery''.] The condition or practices of a warlock; impishness. [Rare.]
{{smaller block|The true mark of ''warlockry''. {{right|''J. Baillie''.}}}}
